the author dr. Richard Dawkins who has  written a God Delusion  as well as The Selfish Gene best-seller  dr. Dawkins why did you choose to write  a autobiography at this point I'm  getting all of it and seemed like a good  moment to do so  my mother is 96 and is a very useful  resource for me to go and talk to tap  her memories and it's been a wonderful  experience actually in effect  interviewing her to write the book  seemed like the right time to do it this  this is in fact just the first half this  is up to the age of 35 up to where I  wrote The Selfish Gene which marks a  sort of natural watershed in my life I  suppose and so if they did make sense to  divide it into two books this is the  first one taking me through childhood  school days University early work as a  scientist and after writing a Selfish  Gene at the age of 35 well why was the  Selfish Gene a natural halfway point for  you in a sense  The Selfish Gene changed my life up but  before that I was at an ordinary  research scientist sort of in a white  lab coat  and then after the self-esteem I stayed  on the faculty at Oxford and went on  teaching and went on doing some research  but I became I suppose more of a public  figure writing books for for large  audiences  who were John and gene Hawkins Dawkins  John and gene were my parents they were  my mother is still alive they my father  was a biologist he did botany at Oxford  so his career began rather similarly to  mine we went to the same kind of  schooling so the fee-paying rather  expensive English SWE's Anglican schools  and then he read botany at Oxford where  as I read serology at Oxford he did he  did research I did research then he went  into the British colonial service which  was the thing did in those days you  don't anymore of course and was posted  to Nyasaland which is now Malawi in  Central East Africa  and then was called up it to fight in  the war against the Italians in  Abyssinia and Somaliland and and I was  born around that time my mother was an  art student came a Cornish family and  they had a shared love of wild things  wild flowers made both of the knew all  the wildflowers and so I was brought up  in an atmosphere of scientific inquiry  and and love of nature were you brought  up in the Anglican Church my parents had  no interest in in with religion but -  central Anakin's foods rather difficult  not to we know that but that's again I  think that normally happened it was  quite hard to find a school that wasn't  in some way churchy and when did you  lose that connection  I suppose finally at the age of about 16  when I was at school but I had my doubts  from earlier I think I first of all had  my doubts around the age of about nine  when my mother wanted out to me that  there were lots of different religions  and when I was brought up in was not  hearing the one so when you asked your  parents about religion about God how did  they respond I think my mother told me  the standard sort of Christian stories  as though she believed them which I  think she didn't and then when I went to  school I got it all from school  Richard Dawkins how did the selfish gene  come about as a book well about ten  years before I wrote it in 1966 I was  asked by my boss the Nobel prize-winning  scientist Niko Tinbergen if I would  stand in and give his lectures for him  he was on sabbatical leave and I wrote a  course of lectures in animal behavior  which pretty much foreshadowed the  rhetoric of The Selfish Gene the idea of  immortal genes funding through the  generations discarding a succession of  bodies all that rhetoric was in those  lectures in 1966 and I vaguely thought  at some point I might write it all down  and I finally did so in 1976  when the self-esteem was published but  you can find in The Selfish Gene almost  the same words as as the lectures that I  gave in 1966 so I don't know why delayed  so long I think I actually started to  put not pen to paper but typewriter key  to paper in about 1972 or 73 when there  was a strike in Britain and and so there  were frequent power cuts we called  electricity cups and I couldn't do my  research so I thought well now's the  time to start writing that long a long  promise book because you didn't need  electricity didn't need electricity in  those days a manual typewriter in an  appetite for wonder you talked about  John Maynard Smith yes who is he who was  he  he was a very wonderful man he was a  very distinguished biologist a very  wonderful character students loved him  he was funny  irreverent constantly talking to  students about real work he didn't do  any of the things that professors do  like fuss about where to where the next  grant is coming from anything like that  he just got on with it and his tools of  the trade were pretty much a pencil and  paper because he was a theorist and he  inspired generations of students he  inspired me although I was never  actually his student and many of his  ideas are incorporated in The Selfish  Gene play a big role in The Selfish Gene  after The Selfish Gene was published how  did your life change as a professor did  you become a celebrity professor in a  sense not immediately it's the book did  sell very well and I think it sold well  over a million copies in total it did  cause a bit of a sensation and I did  find myself being invited to do lots of  things which I haven't been before I  suppose I'm talking to people like you I  suppose that that did start to happen  then and I suppose it did it did change  my life and set me on a new course of  writing other other books after that  here in the States  you're quite well known not only as a  scientist but also as an atheist when  did you start writing about that in  earnest The Selfish Gene has um  intimations the tabs and it doesn't turn  conceal the cat and I suppose all my  books I mean the the next book I wrote  for a popular audience which was the  blind watchmaker is all about the  argument from design which is still I  think the dominant reason why most  people will give for believing in a  Supreme Being they will say look at the  world of nature look at the trees look  at the birds over the flowers it's all  too complicated to her about my chance  and of course it is too complicated to  combine pie charts that's not what it's  about it came about by natural selection  which is the opposite of chance and so  the blind watchmaker  it was an attempt to explain that people  and was taken as an atheistic book by  many people in either  I think the subtitle in America was  something like why the evidence of  evolution shows a universe without  designer something of that sort so the  blind watchmaker was at least  interpreted as an atheistic book and all  my other books since then could be  interpreted in the same way the only  book that is explicitly and at length  atheistic is The God Delusion published  in 2006 which is I think that's my  biggest seller in fact that that's sold  more than 2 million copies in in English  and I don't know how many and searches  foreign languages but apart from that my  books haven't been devoted to atheism  and the way that the God Delusion has  Charles Darwin your hero who saved in an  appetite for one indeed yes and Charles  Darwin was a brilliant thinker brilliant  explainer one of the things I find  surprising is that Darwin's idea which  is really so simple and when anybody  should be able to understand it came so  late  it came two hundred years after Newton  and really you might think that knew  what Newton did was was was cleverer  more difficult to think of in inventing  calculus working out optics  understanding gravity the laws of  mechanics the laws of motion these are  all supreme achievements of the human  mind  two hundred years before Darwin there  you can tell wondering why somebody like  new theorists or indeed Aristotle didn't  think of Darwin's really simple but  immensely powerful idea  Richard Dawkins as somebody who's  written about science written about  ideas what was it like to write about  yourself quite difficult I this a cruel  embarrassment factor in writing about  herself and I was persuaded by both  British and American publishers that it  was worth doing is I should do it since  i should overcome that embarrassment  factor I hope it's a good-humoured book  I like to think it's humorous I like to  think there are plenty of laughs in it  and finally I can say I think that I  enjoyed writing it did you end up  enjoying it yes I did what reliving  memories it's not exactly a systematic  history of my life it's more of a  patchwork of memories which actually was  the working title that I had in mind  that I started writing in so they're  kind of random memories but I hope that  random memories are put together in such  a way and I hope a funny way as to  encapsulate her life it'll be in the  books stores in September of 2013 is  this the American cover or is this the  English and American no that's the  that's the American cover and what is  the English cover luckily the British  cover is sideways on view it looks it's  not smiling it's sort of I suppose you  could say it's more  poetic rather than cheerful and it has  me holding up a little jar containing an  insect and gazing at this of this  infants inspecting this this insect why  why different covers why would that sell  in England and I have no idea publishers  have their ways and I actually like both  covers that I'm quite happy to have both  of them out there an appetite for wonder  the making of a scientists Richard  Dawkins is the author it'll be in  bookstores in September of 2013 this is  book TV on c-span to watch more from
 

